# Checkers variants

The player who sets up a checkers game picks the variant. Both players see its rules before the game starts, and on screen throughout it (the rule tags under the player names, and the Rules button).

| Variant | Men capture backwards | Kings | Capturing | A man crowned mid-capture | First move |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| American | no | one square | compulsory, any capture | stops there | Black |
| Russian | yes | fly | compulsory, any capture | carries on as a king | Red |
| Brazilian | yes | fly | compulsory, must take the most | carries on as a man, crowned only if it ends there | Red |
| Pool | yes | fly | compulsory, any capture | carries on as a man, crowned only if it ends there | Black |
| House | chosen at the table | | | | |

## Rules every variant shares

- A capture goes on while there is more to take.
- Taken pieces come off when the move ends. A piece cannot be jumped twice, and a flying king cannot pass over one it has just taken.
- A player with no pieces left, or who cannot move, loses. House rules can make a blocked player draw instead.
- The game is drawn when the same position comes up **Draw by repetition** times, or after **Draw after quiet moves** moves each with no capture and no man moved.

## Playing a capture

Players pick a capture one landing square at a time. When a capture can go two ways, the player chooses the way. F (or a right-click) takes back the last jump before the move is sent. When a capture is compulsory, the pieces that can take are ringed.

## Notation

American and Pool games are written with the squares numbered 1 to 32 (11-15, 22x15x8). Russian and Brazilian games use board letters (c3-d4, c3:e5:g7). House rules let the player choose.
